= Semantics — Technical =

== Interpretation ==
For a formal language L, semantics normally specifies a class of interpretations/models and a mapping from syntactic expressions to semantic values.

Model-theoretically:

<pre>
I ⊨ φ
</pre>

states that interpretation I satisfies formula φ.

Logical entailment can then be defined:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

iff every interpretation satisfying Γ also satisfies φ.

== Denotation and intension ==
A semantic theory may distinguish:
* reference/denotation;
* sense/intension;
* extension;
* proposition;
* possible-world content.

Co-referring terms can differ in cognitive or semantic role, motivating distinctions beyond reference alone.

== Compositional semantics ==
Let <code>⟦e⟧</code> denote the semantic value of expression e.

A compositional system gives rules such that:

<pre>
⟦complex expression⟧
= F(⟦part1⟧, ..., ⟦partn⟧, syntactic structure)
</pre>

The function F depends on the construction.

== Operational / denotational semantics ==
Programming-language semantics commonly includes:

'''Operational semantics''' — behavior defined through execution/transition rules.

'''Denotational semantics''' — constructs mapped to mathematical objects.

'''Axiomatic semantics''' — logical assertions characterize program behavior.

These are different semantic techniques, not different syntaxes.

== Semantic consequence versus procedure ==
Entailment is defined semantically.

An inference engine is an algorithm attempting to derive entailed conclusions.

Soundness means derived conclusions are semantically valid.

Completeness means every semantically valid consequence in scope can, in principle, be derived.

== RDF semantics ==
RDF semantics gives interpretations to RDF graphs and defines entailment regimes.

The string used as an IRI does not by itself fix all social-world meaning. Formal semantics constrains model-theoretic interpretation; community usage still matters for what identifiers are intended to denote.

